17592186045443 true] [17592186480007 1083 #uuid "0c81a280-e468-5bb1-ae24-06ecd33d692c" 13194139535210 17592186045443 true] [17592186480008 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480012 13194139535210 17592186045443 true] [17592186480008 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535210 17592186045443 true] [17592186480008 1071 "Given that $f(x)=- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}-8$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=-ln⁡3$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139535210 17592186045443 true] [17592186480008 1072 #uuid "24285fdc-6262-5d37-b204-181d4efdc6b1" 13194139535210 17592186045443 true] [17592186480008 1074 "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $-8$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}-8) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-8) \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-ln⁡3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-ln⁡3} & ={(- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=-ln⁡3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-ln⁡3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535210 17592186045443 true] [17592186480009 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480033 13194139535210 17592186045443 true] [17592186480009 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535210 17592186045443 true] [17592186480009 1071 "Given that $f(x) = \\ln 2 + 2e^{x}$, find $f^{′}(x)$." 13194139535210 17592186045443 true] [17592186480009 1072 #uuid "27963039-7ab9-50a1-a524-335dda2120d3" 13194139535210 17592186045443 true] [17592186480009 1074 "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $ln⁡2$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nUsing the addition rule, then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡2+2{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡2)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =0+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535210 17592186045443 true] [17592186480010 1080 "e" 13194139535210 17592186045443 true] [17592186480010 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535210 17592186045443 true] [17592186480010 1083 #uuid "31e88a41-78ca-580b-a312-79116f88663a" 13194139535210 17592186045443 true] [17592186480011 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480042 13194139535210 17592186045443 true] [17592186480011 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535210 17592186045443 true] [17592186480011 1071 "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}-11$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139535210 17592186045443 true] [17592186480011 1072 #uuid "3368f5c9-3775-5d06-9195-e0e05c5824a2" 13194139535210 17592186045443 true] [17592186480011 1074 "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $-11$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}-11) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-11) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}} & ={(\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535210 17592186045443 true] [17592186480012 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480007 13194139535210 17592186045443 true] [17592186480012 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139535210 17592186045443 true] [17592186480012 1077 "field-1" 13194139535210 17592186045443 true] [17592186480012 1078 #edb/ref 17592186480007 13